WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:01.560
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Guillaume.

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.667
I heard you help companies
move to the cloud.

00:00:04.667 --> 00:00:06.000
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE: Hi, Martin.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.010
Yes, I work for Accenture.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.410
We're [INAUDIBLE] company.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.460
We help businesses move to the
cloud, including serverless.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.250
I have helped many
businesses make the move.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:19.300
MARTIN OMANDER: Great.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.210
Could you tell us what you've
learned about successful cloud

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:22.717
migrations?

00:00:22.717 --> 00:00:23.800
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE: Yeah.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:24.790
I would be happy to.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:34.767
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:34.767 --> 00:00:35.850
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.670
So in your experience,
what kind of businesses

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.420
want to move to the cloud?

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.520
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE:
Oh, most of them.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.930
They don't want to buy more
servers for their data centers.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:49.410
Servers in the cloud are cheaper
when you take into account

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.420
all the management costs--

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.440
plugs in servers, unplug
this one, destroy the disk,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.170
ensure the site
physical security.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.020
[INAUDIBLE] has released a great
video on the Google Cloud data

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:04.838
center security, and it's
harder than you think.

00:01:04.838 --> 00:01:05.880
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, yeah.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.320
That's a really good video.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.840
You know, I remember
putting servers

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.530
into racks for one of my
startups back in the day.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.985
Our data center was in
San Mateo, California.

00:01:15.985 --> 00:01:17.610
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE:
Yeah, that's fine.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.360
But you may need one data
center in Europe, one in Asia,

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.180
one in North America, and so on.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.850
Loads of data storage
may force you to do that.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.630
Or you may want your
servers close to your users

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.500
to reduce latency.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.280
This means it's
hard to go global.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.020
With Cloud, you
get that for free.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.200
But there are true challenges.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.470
In my experience, when a
business migrate to the cloud,

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.990
they face three
major challenges.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:49.150
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, what
are those three challenges?

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.570
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE:
First of all, security.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.870
The security teams in most
businesses want to make sure

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.990
that their internal system
cannot be accessed from

00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:59.700
the internet.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:04.090
That's usually how they're
existing on-prem [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:05.910
MARTIN OMANDER: OK,
that sounds reasonable.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.767
How do you help them with that?

00:02:07.767 --> 00:02:09.100
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE: Two things.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:12.540
First, I like to remove the
virtual machines to VPCs

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.350
and to put all the VMs
be on load balancers.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:20.310
So the VMs are not directly
visible to the [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:21.490
internet.

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:25.740
Second, I help them set
up oganization policies.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:29.610
This means that new VMs
cannot get [INAUDIBLE] without

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.250
an exception to the policy.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:33.340
MARTIN OMANDER: I see.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.860
So that's for virtual machines.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.903
What about security
for serverless?

00:02:38.903 --> 00:02:41.070
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE:
Serverless is a new [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:44.800
Many serverless products
have [INAUDIBLE] by default,

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.480
so they base their security
on cloud [INAUDIBLE]..

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.410
And many security teams are
uncomfortable with that.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.370
MARTIN OMANDER: And how
do you deal with that?

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.910
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE: Oh, I
explain this to [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:00.670
and how we can help them
implement zero-trust.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:03.270
This means that we don't
trust incoming traffic

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:06.600
just because it's from
your internal network.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.110
You check all traffic
and trust the identity

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:13.000
in [INAUDIBLE] of the origin.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.420
MARTIN OMANDER: So you said
there were three challenges.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.350
What's the second one?

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:20.040
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE: The
second is cost uncertainty.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.070
It's hard to estimate how
much your cloud system will

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:26.697
cost when exposed to [INAUDIBLE]
workload and traffic.

00:03:26.697 --> 00:03:29.280
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, yeah, I've
heard that from developers too.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:30.960
And what's your solution?

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.410
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE: I tell our
customers set up [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:37.410
[INAUDIBLE] with
a cost calculator.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.170
That way, they get an
email if [INAUDIBLE] bill

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.930
goes above a certain threshold.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:46.410
However, many of my customer
have gone serverless.

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:49.110
That's magic for them,
that scale up and down

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:50.440
automatically.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:54.060
But they're also the feeling
of losing control of cost.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.730
MARTIN OMANDER: Right, and
what can be done about that?

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.230
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE:
In general, you

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.750
can set the maximum
number of instances.

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:05.100
So, for example,
[INAUDIBLE],, they

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:09.000
will never pay for more than
10 [INAUDIBLE] instances.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.250
And they can put these
limits on several

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.820
of their serverless
products, like data flow or

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:15.631
[? bitcoin. ?]

00:04:15.631 --> 00:04:16.589
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:18.300
And what's the third
challenge you've

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.640
seen businesses run into when
they migrate to the cloud?

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:23.880
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE: Yeah,
this other one is trust.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:28.390
Everyone-- as a customer, I have
to trust the cloud provider.

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:31.740
It's easy when your system
are in your own data centers.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.830
You trust your own
servers, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:37.980
monitoring systems, and
when you know the teams

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:40.140
in charge of your data centers.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:43.082
MARTIN OMANDER: And how do you
address that trust challenge?

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:45.540
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE: Businesses
have to ask the same thing.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.180
Can my cloud provider
[INAUDIBLE] server better

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:52.598
than I can, or as well
as me, but for less?

00:04:52.598 --> 00:04:54.390
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
that's why businesses

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.813
want to move to the cloud
in the first place, right?

00:04:56.813 --> 00:04:58.230
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE:
Right, right.

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:02.070
Smart companies want to
delegate IT to a cloud provider,

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:05.130
to save money and to
be more efficient.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:08.220
They want to focus their
teams and money on their core

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:12.510
business, but it takes
time to gain trust.

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:16.710
And it's not a matter of company
size or move to cloud budgets.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:19.890
It depends more on
how old the system is.

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.170
The older it is,
the harder it will

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:25.200
be to gain confidence
in the cloud.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.050
One good way is to
run a pilot project

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:33.175
to migrate one small
noncritical system to the cloud.

00:05:33.175 --> 00:05:34.800
MARTIN OMANDER: And
when your customers

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:38.005
run these pilot projects,
how does it usually work out?

00:05:38.005 --> 00:05:41.130
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE: [CHUCKLES]
In fact, most of them

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:43.530
want to be cloud
native, to use only

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:45.900
managed and serviced
products, or to use

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.330
containers and Kubernetes.

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:51.330
However, it's a big
step to train the teams

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.550
in serverless or Kubernetes.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:56.370
You need many small
steps before that,

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:59.250
like setting up
your CI/CD pipeline

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:02.916
and putting your
applications in containers.

00:06:02.916 --> 00:06:04.920
MARTIN OMANDER: CI/CD
pipelines and containers--

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.530
those are big topics.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:08.990
How can they get started?

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:11.630
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE: I tell them
to start by performing a lift

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:13.190
and shift migration.

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:16.070
Move one of their existing
application, as is,

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:17.510
to the cloud.

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:21.560
As they do that, they learn
what's works well in the cloud.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:25.490
And they are in a better
place with an existing app

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:30.272
to set up CI/CD and modernize
with serverless or Kubernetes.

00:06:30.272 --> 00:06:31.730
MARTIN OMANDER:
And why should they

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:35.940
start using CI/CD and
serverless or Kubernetes?

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:37.460
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE:
Oh, they can

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.250
shift code faster,
reduce so many errors,

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:42.320
and optimize costs.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:44.390
[INAUDIBLE] and the
Google Cloud white paper

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:47.240
describe the return
on investment

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.940
of DevOps transformation
and how to do it.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.250
Have a look at it.

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:53.480
MARTIN OMANDER: Sounds good.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:55.910
Thanks, for sharing your
hard-won experience with us

00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:57.110
today, Guillaume.

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:59.180
If you have any questions
for Guillaume or me,

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:01.410
please enter them in
the comments below.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:02.990
Also, please let
me know if there

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:05.870
are any other topics you'd like
us to bring up on the show.

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:06.830
Until next time.

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:08.980
[MUSIC PLAYING]

